  "content": {
    "id": "p28-q9-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "9",
    "prompt": "Find the point on the graph of $8x+6y=25$ closest to the circle of radius $1$ centred at $(0,0)$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\left(2,\\frac32\\right)$.\n\nIntersect the given line with the perpendicular through the circle centre.",
      "worked": "The line has gradient $-\\frac43$. The perpendicular through the centre has equation $y=\\frac34x$.\nSubstitute into the given line:\n$$\\begin{align*}8x+6\\left(\\frac34x\\right)&=25\\\\\\frac{25}{2}x&=25\\\\x&=2\\\\y&=\\frac34(2)\\\\y&=\\frac32.\\end{align*}$$\nThe distance of this point from the centre is\n$$\\begin{align*}d&=\\sqrt{2^2+\\left(\\frac32\\right)^2}\\\\d&=\\frac52>1.\\end{align*}$$\nThe line lies outside the circle. For a point on the line, its distance to the circle is its distance from the centre minus $1$, so the perpendicular projection $\\left(2,\\frac32\\right)$ is also closest to the circle.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 126
  },
